WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.633
Hi, I'm Inko! Look closely at any screen
and you'll find a grid of tiny squares:

2
00:00:05.633 --> 00:00:10.267
pixels. To paint a picture,
a shader has to know where each one sits.

3
00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.867
So every pixel needs an address.

4
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:16.700
Today we'll meet the three ways
graphics writes that address down:

5
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.433
pixel coordinates, UV, and NDC.

6
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:26.633
Start with a number line:
one axis, one number.

7
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:30.600
Add a second axis at a right angle,
pick an origin where they cross,

8
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.700
and any point becomes a pair, x and y.

9
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.433
Walk x steps across, then y steps up.

10
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:43.000
Add a third axis, z, at right
angles to both, and you're in 3D.

11
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:46.667
Nothing forces the axes to be
perpendicular and evenly spaced,

12
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.267
but every graphics tool
quietly assumes they are.

13
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.133
Which axis points up?

14
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:58.067
In three.js, and in the
glTF format, y points up.

15
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.533
Blender uses z-up instead.

16
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:03.567
Bring a Blender character into
three.js without converting,

17
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:06.933
and it lies along z, head toward you.

18
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.167
The second question is handedness.

19
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:13.967
In a right-handed system, curl your
right hand's fingers from x toward y,

20
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:16.467
and your thumb points along z.

21
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:22.067
Three.js is right-handed; DirectX and
Unity are traditionally left-handed.

22
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:26.433
Mix them up, and your model comes
out mirrored, or even inside out.

23
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:29.767
Even flat screens disagree:
math puts y up,

24
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:34.200
but a 2D canvas starts at the
top left and counts y downward.

25
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:36.967
Now, textures.

26
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:41.033
A texture might be sixteen pixels
wide, or over eight thousand.

27
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:42.967
UV space ignores that.

28
00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:48.267
One corner is (0, 0),
the opposite corner is (1, 1),

29
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:50.467
whatever the image size.

30
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.533
u runs across, v runs up or down.

31
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:57.900
So a shader samples by UV and
never needs the pixel count.

32
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.133
Swap in a bigger texture, and not
one line of shader code changes.

33
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:06.433
One catch: images put v
equals zero at the top,

34
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:10.133
but classic WebGL textures
put it at the bottom.

35
00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:11.567
Only v differs,

36
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:15.033
so a wrongly flipped texture
comes out flipped top to bottom,

37
00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.933
never left to right.

38
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:20.733
So how does a pixel find its UV?

39
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:24.267
Take a tiny screen,
four pixels wide and two tall.

40
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.300
A pixel isn't a point.

41
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.167
It's a little square, and its index
marks the square's edge, not its center.

42
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:33.933
The center sits half a pixel further in.

43
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:37.433
So: add a half, then divide by the width.

44
00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:40.700
u = (x + 0.5) / W.

45
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.700
v works the same way, with the height.

46
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.133
Inko has eight tentacles,
so each one takes a pixel,

47
00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:50.133
and they all run the same
little formula at once.

48
00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:56.133
That's how a GPU works: one tiny program,
run for every pixel, in parallel.

49
00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.833
Inside a shader,

50
00:02:57.833 --> 00:03:03.067
gl_FragCoord already sits at the pixel
center and starts at the bottom left,

51
00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:06.267
so there you simply
divide by the resolution.

52
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.700
UV runs from 0 to 1.

53
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.400
That's perfect for textures,

54
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.600
but for directions we want
a range centered on zero:

55
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.667
−1 to 1.

56
00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:21.800
WebGL calls it NDC,
normalized device coordinates.

57
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.267
Now the sign alone
tells you left or right.

58
00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:30.267
To get there, stretch by two,
then shift by one: x = 2u − 1.

59
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:32.633
Just subtracting a half isn't enough.

60
00:03:32.633 --> 00:03:35.600
Your drawing would fill only
the middle half of the screen.

61
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.100
And y = 1 − 2v, because v
grows downward in images,

62
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.433
while NDC grows upward.

63
00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:48.733
So: pixel coordinates count
squares from the top left.

64
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:53.667
UV squeezes any size into 0 to 1,
measured at pixel centers.

65
00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:58.600
NDC stretches that to −1 to 1,
centered on the screen.

66
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.533
Watch for the two classic slips:
forgetting the half pixel,

67
00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:04.533
and forgetting to flip y.

68
00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:09.300
Now open the UV explorer in this lesson,
and see which corner turns yellow.

69
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.433
See you in the next lesson!
